The case {#sec1-2}
========

A 38/female patient, presented to the outpatient department in the Obstetrics and Gynaecology department for management of severe lower abdominal pain. She had suffered from intermittent, lower abdominal and pelvic pain and had passed two small pieces of gravel during passing of a stool. Physical examination was unremarkable except for a small bulge in the lower abdomen. During examination, the uterus moved during Valsalva\'s manoeuvre.

Haemorrhoids were suspected and digital rectoscopy examination revealed a circular lesion of size 1×1 cm in the rectum of 6cm from anal verge. Rectal biopsy was taken which were non-specific and biopsies were sent for microbiological examination including for tuberculous material.

